  "name": "FLUID ALPHA FETOPROTEIN (AFP)",
  "code": "1086",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"127\" data-end=\"448\"><strong data-start=\"127\" data-end=\"154\">Alpha-Fetoprotein (AFP)</strong> is a glycoprotein normally produced by the fetal liver and yolk sac. While it's commonly tested in <strong data-start=\"254\" data-end=\"263\">serum</strong>, measuring <strong data-start=\"275\" data-end=\"297\">AFP in body fluids</strong> (e.g., <strong data-start=\"305\" data-end=\"323\">amniotic fluid</strong>, <strong data-start=\"325\" data-end=\"342\">pleural fluid</strong>, <strong data-start=\"344\" data-end=\"361\">ascitic fluid</strong>, or <strong data-start=\"366\" data-end=\"380\">cyst fluid</strong>) can provide diagnostic information in specific clinical scenarios.</p>\n<p data-start=\"450\" data-end=\"476\"><strong data-start=\"450\" data-end=\"476\">Clinical uses include:</strong></p>\n<ul data-start=\"478\" data-end=\"875\">\n<li data-start=\"478\" data-end=\"625\">\n<p data-start=\"480\" data-end=\"625\"><strong data-start=\"480\" data-end=\"502\">Amniotic fluid AFP</strong>: Screening for <strong data-start=\"518\" data-end=\"546\">open neural tube defects</strong> (e.g., spina bifida, anencephaly) and <strong data-start=\"585\" data-end=\"611\">abdominal wall defects</strong> in the fetus.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"626\" data-end=\"792\">\n<p data-start=\"628\" data-end=\"792\"><strong data-start=\"628\" data-end=\"664\">Pleural/ascitic/cystic fluid AFP</strong>: Evaluation of <strong data-start=\"680\" data-end=\"696\">malignancies</strong>, especially when hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC), germ cell tumors, or metastases are suspected.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"793\" data-end=\"875\">\n<p data-start=\"795\" data-end=\"875\"><strong data-start=\"795\" data-end=\"806\">CSF AFP</strong> (rare): May be used in research or rare pediatric tumor assessments.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Clinical Context</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Elevated AFP Suggests</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Amniotic fluid (second trimester)</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Neural tube defects</strong>, <strong>abdominal wall defects</strong>, fetal demise, or ventral wall anomalies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ascitic or pleural fluid</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Hepatocellular carcinoma</strong> rupture or seeding, <strong>germ cell tumor</strong> involvement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ovarian/testicular cyst fluid</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Yolk sac tumor</strong> or <strong>mixed germ cell tumor</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CSF (very rare use)</strong></td>\n<td>May be elevated in <strong>intracranial germ cell tumors</strong> with yolk sac elements</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
